  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\n### Title\nFeature Request: Native scrubbing thumbnail preview support for `<video>` controls (e.g., via `<track kind=\"thumbnails\">` or declarative attribute)\n\n### Problem / Use Case\nCurrently, modern video platforms widely offer **timeline scrubbing thumbnail previews** (displaying a preview frame or sprite image when hovering or scrubbing along the progress bar) to help users quickly locate specific scenes in a video.\n\nHowever, the native `<video controls>` UI across browsers lacks a standardized mechanism to opt into or provide scrubbing previews. As a result:\n1. Developers are forced to abandon native `<video controls>` and build heavy custom JavaScript players just to support thumbnail scrubbing, losing built-in browser accessibility, PiP, casting, and native mobile controls.\n2. Users viewing raw video files or lightweight pages using native `<video controls>` cannot quickly preview timestamps without seeking and triggering extra buffering.\n\n<img width=\"1280\" height=\"938\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/04810c35-c607-4bcc-ab67-0bb6cb2aad59\" />\n\n### Proposed Direction (Open for Discussion)\nExplore a standardized, opt-in mechanism in HTML for native `<video>` controls to display scrubbing previews, such as:\n* **Option A (Declarative `<track>` integration):** Standardize `kind=\"thumbnails\"` (or `kind=\"metadata\"` with a recognized WebVTT sprite/image convention, similar to existing patterns in third-party players like Video.js / JW Player) so user agents can natively parse VTT time ranges and image URLs/media fragments (`#xywh=...`) to render thumbnails during timeline scrubbing.\n* **Option B (Auto-generated frame preview opt-in):** Allow user agents to optionally generate scrubbing previews from already-buffered video frames when an attribute (e.g., `scrubbingpreview` or similar) is present, avoiding unexpected network overhead.\n\n### Key Considerations\n* **Bandwidth & Performance:** Fetching unbuffered frames over the network on hover could waste bandwidth or cause decoding jank. Relying on a dedicated VTT thumbnail track (Option A) or strictly buffered ranges (Option B) mitigates unnecessary network requests.\n* **Privacy & Cross-Origin:** Thumbnail image URLs referenced in `<track>` should respect existing CORS (`crossorigin`) policies on `<video>`.\n* **Accessibility & Touch Devices:** Touch scrubbing (drag-and-hold on timeline) can also benefit from visual preview feedback, while screen readers can continue announcing timestamp/chapter cues without extra noise.\n* **Browser UI Freedom:** The specification would define the data source and opt-in semantics, leaving the exact popover layout, sizing, and animation up to each user agent's native media controls.\n\n### Prior Art / Ecosystem Context\n* WebVTT thumbnail sprites using `#xywh=x,y,w,h` spatial media fragments are already a de facto industry convention across web media players.\n* Desktop media players and major streaming platforms treat timeline scrubbing previews as a baseline usability feature.\n\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\n_No response_\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\n_No response_\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
